<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Canandaigua</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1794</span> · Also known as Pickering Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Canandaigua was signed on 11 November 1794 at Canandaigua, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, represented by Timothy Pickering, and the Six Nations of the Iroquois. It established peace and friendship, recognised the lands of the Seneca and other nations in New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, and promised an annual payment in goods. That payment, including bolts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth, is still made each year, and the Haudenosaunee regard the treaty as in force.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-canandaigua.jpg" alt="Treaty of Canandaigua" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ben Nolan (cosmictraveler photoblog on Flickr) · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Courthouse_Rock,_Canandaigua_NY.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
